WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.639
All right, in this problem, an engineer is presented with a vertical curve upon which

00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:08.279
car accident has recently occurred.

00:00:08.279 --> 00:00:14.640
The engineer is asked to determine the tangent slope of the road at station 17 plus 0, 0,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.160
where the skid marks of a given car began.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:27.039
So in this problem, we are being asked to find a slope at a station in a horizontal, or

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:29.679
I'm sorry, in a vertical curve.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:34.640
So if we type in vertical curve into our reference manual to get us started, that's

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.119
going to take us to chapter five titled transportation, specifically directly to section

00:00:40.119 --> 00:00:45.560
5.3.1 symmetrical vertical curve formula.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.280
So we're going to use the equations there to solve this problem.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:58.039
The first thing we need to solve for is the rate of change along the horizontal curve.

00:00:58.039 --> 00:01:04.519
With that term, the rate of vertical curvature, otherwise thought of as the rate of change

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:13.439
of grade, is given as k equals L over a.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:17.719
And in this case, k is the rate of vertical change.

00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:23.599
L is going to be the length of the curve, otherwise known here as the distance between

00:01:23.599 --> 00:01:25.480
the two stations.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.560
And then A is going to be the algebraic difference in grades.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.680
So the way we can write this out, I'll write down A really fast.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:42.439
That's going to be g2 minus g1 absolute value.

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:45.120
And this is expressed as a percent.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.719
g2 in this case is going to be the grade of our forward tangent.

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:57.959
And grade one is going to be the rate of our backward tangent or back tangent.

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:01.920
So let's get started calculating k.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:10.840
That's simply going to be 19 hundred feet, because remember, we can rewrite stations

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:28.180
in this terminology instead minus 1400 feet over 2.9 minus a negative 2.5 percent, which

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:36.400
is somewhat redundant, since this is going to be an absolute value here.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:44.360
And if we solve this out, we can see that our rate of vertical curvature is going to

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:50.439
be 92.59.

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:56.319
And if we want, we can write this down as feet per percent.

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:02.159
I don't know if this is actually the way it's written out in transportation engineering,

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:04.560
because that's not my own personal background.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.819
But I do find that writing out units in a problem can help you solve, help you remember

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:12.219
what you're trying to solve for at the end.

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:15.080
So I personally like to do it in this case.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:22.439
So we have found k, the rate of our vertical curvature from one station to the next, otherwise

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:28.560
known as the rate of change to the grade over distance.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:41.520
So what we can do now is find our slope at station 17 plus 0, 0.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.599
So we can wind up using this exact same equation again, and simply solve for a different

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:48.640
term.

00:03:48.639 --> 00:04:01.239
So in this case, g2 is going to be the new term we're solving for since station 17 plus

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:06.959
0, 0 is going to be the new term, the new station that we've been given.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:20.280
So in this case, what we can say, since we already solved for k, we can say 92.59 feet per

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:36.439
percent equals 1700 feet minus 1400 feet over g2 is now the grade that we're solving for

00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:42.839
minus negative 2.5.

00:04:42.839 --> 00:04:49.480
And down here, that's going to be a percent absolute value.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:05:00.040
So if we write this out, we could solve for this algebraically and say 92.59 feet over

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:08.960
percent times term g2 plus since negative 2, negative 0 is going to give us a positive.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:23.000
If we multiply those 2 terms together, we're going to get 231.48 feet over percent times

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.000
per cent.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.480
So just feet equals the 300 feet on the other side.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:36.680
So we can see that we can subtract these because the units work out.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:52.800
So that's going to give us 92.59 feet over percent equals 68.52 feet.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:59.800
And if we were to divide now, oh, I forgot to add term g2 in here.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:15.280
So if we were to isolate g2 now, we will get g2 equals 0.74 feet over feet over percent,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:21.480
which is really just percent.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:28.000
So the answer to our problem is the second answer here.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.040
And that's all it takes to solve this problem.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.800
One more thing I want to point out is that there is technically a second way to solve

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:45.560
this problem using some additional information that's provided in the reference manual.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.439
So I don't use it because it's not explicitly called out as clearly.

00:06:50.439 --> 00:07:01.160
And it relies on the fact that you know that the derivative of an elevation is a slope.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.680
So again, I think the first way is easier to use.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.439
But let's go through the second way and just in case it makes more sense for some of

00:07:09.439 --> 00:07:10.439
you.

00:07:10.519 --> 00:07:16.680
So in the same part of the reference manual where we got our previous equations,

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:23.759
you'll see that there is a curve elevation equation provided.

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:26.439
And I can write that out here.

00:07:26.439 --> 00:07:37.399
So it's given as y point of vertical curve plus g1 times x plus.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.240
A times x squared.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:59.680
Now in this case, A is also given in the equation in the handbook as g2 minus g1 over 2l.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:15.840
So to use this equation in this problem, again, we have to know that the derivative

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:27.280
of elevation is a slope.

00:08:27.279 --> 00:08:36.799
And that's not explicitly called out in the reference manual.

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:39.799
So that's just something to be aware of.

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:48.480
That being said, let's see what happens when we take the derivative of this formula.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:54.120
So the first thing that we'd see is we can rewrite it over here first.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:05.879
Y pvc plus g1 times x plus will combine these two parts now.

00:09:05.879 --> 00:09:16.920
G2 minus g1 over 2l times x squared.

00:09:16.919 --> 00:09:34.079
So if we were to take the derivative of this, first we'll see that this term is gone.

00:09:34.079 --> 00:09:40.959
And x will be derived away leaving us just with g1.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:56.960
And this x squared would become 2 times g2 minus g1 over 2l times x.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:03.800
And both these two would cancel.

00:10:03.799 --> 00:10:16.439
Let's take us with only, take us down a little more.

00:10:16.439 --> 00:10:29.399
Y prime equals g1 plus g2 minus g1 times x.

00:10:29.399 --> 00:10:34.840
And I forgot to mention that x, as called out in the reference manual here, is going

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:40.919
to be your horizontal distance from your point of vertical curvature to the point on the

00:10:40.919 --> 00:10:43.240
curve that you're interested in.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:54.600
So again, in our case, that's going to be 1700 minus the first point, which was 1400.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:07.560
So Y prime equals negative 0.025.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:13.080
So in this equation, these are expressed as decimals rather than the full percent.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:26.120
Plus 0.029 minus a negative 0.025.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:42.240
These are the grades from given above over 1900 plus subtracted from 1400.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:55.720
Sorry, 1400 subtracted from 1900.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:12:05.519
And x is going to be, like we just said, the distance from the PVC to the point on the curve.

00:12:05.519 --> 00:12:12.000
So that's going to be 1700 minus 1400.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:27.399
And if we were to solve all of this out, you get 0.0074 or 0.74%.

00:12:27.399 --> 00:12:32.799
So this might feel like a faster way to solve the problem, but you do have to inherently

00:12:32.799 --> 00:12:38.919
understand and remember that the derivative of the curve elevation formula will give you

00:12:38.919 --> 00:12:41.360
slope.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:45.759
And that's not something that's explicitly called out in the reference manual, or if it

00:12:45.759 --> 00:12:49.360
is, I missed it myself when I looked through it.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.759
But yeah, that's the alternative way to solve this problem.

00:12:52.759 --> 00:12:58.519
If you don't want to do it that way, there's always the original that we did here.

00:12:58.519 --> 00:12:59.879
And that should be it for this problem.
